string-accumulator — the Tier-1 obligation for
src/transforms/string_accumulator.rs
=======================================================================

    isabelle build -d proofs/StringAccumulator -b StringAccumulator

STATUS: checked — 18 lemmas, quick_and_dirty = false, ~1 min from a warm
HOL image. One negative control run and failed (see below).

What is proved (StringAccumulator.thy)

  §1-2  the READ WALKS. mirror_read_walk is the Rust
        read_walk / expr_read_walk / arith_read_walk triple, one constructor
        per arm, no wildcard — the same shape as the code, which is why the
        Arith arm had to be added in BOTH places at once.

  §3-4  the pre-fix walk (v0_expr_reads, with the catch-all arms) and the
        bug report's own witness:

          the_fix_is_load_bearing
            expr_reads (Arith (AVar "tsv")) "tsv"  /\
            ~ v0_expr_reads (Arith (AVar "tsv")) "tsv"

        i.e. `n=$(( tsv + 0 ))` IS a read, and the pre-fix walk did not see
        it. That is BUGS_FOUND.md #11 as a theorem rather than a repro.

  §5    carrier completeness — a read buried in a redirect, a subshell, a
        block, a background job, a pipeline stage, a function body (and its
        named blocks), a ForInit body, a try body, a try EXCEPT clause, a
        case clause body, a case PATTERN, a thunk, a comprehension or an
        arith index key is still found.

  §6    the position rule: before_any_read ss n ==> ~ stmts_read ss n, i.e.
        if no statement before the self-append reads the variable, then the
        append is read #0 of the iteration — the condition under which the
        join-at-read lowering is faithful. a_body_that_reads_after_the_append_
        breaks_the_rule is the contrapositive shape.

The negative control

  Replacing the fixed Arith arm with the pre-fix `False` makes the session
  FAIL at the_fix_is_load_bearing ("Failed to finish proof"). Restored, it is
  green. A session that only ever passes is not evidence.

What is NOT claimed

  * The LOWERING (push-per-append, one join per post-loop read) is statement
    and runtime semantics — Tier 3, unchanged by this file.
  * The C length mirror's vetoes (float/numeric/bigint/env/nameref/...): they
    are consumers' own gates, not part of this verdict.
  * mentions (the raw-text scan) is primitive: the byte-wise identifier-
    boundary scan in text_mentions_var is not modelled, only relied on
    conservatively (a hit refuses, a miss cannot make the verdict wrong when
    the operand is really text).
  * The mirror is VERBATIM-in-shape, not mechanically extracted. The Rust
    side carries the executable half: walk_tests::
    an_arith_read_of_the_accumulator_refuses_the_verdict (verified failing-
    first by restoring the catch-all), the_same_loop_without_the_arith_read_
    still_fires (the control), an_arith_write_through_incdec_refuses_the_body,
    carriers_do_not_hide_reads, continue_does_not_refuse_the_body.
